<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nyau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gule Wamkulu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nyau is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chewa">Chewa</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, whose initiated men perform masked dances at funerals, initiations and memorial rites. The dancers represent ancestral spirits and animals, and the society historically balanced the power of chiefs. Its ritual dance, Gule Wamkulu, is recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as intangible cultural heritage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nyau.jpg" alt="Nyau" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Hillewaert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chewa_mask_-_Malawi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
